Herpes Simplex Virus (including Genital Herpes)
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Painful clustered vesicles on an erythematous base, Shallow weeping ulcerations, Localized tingling/burning prodrome, Tender regional lymphadenopathy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-019
Reason for Visit: Sudden, agonizing genital pain, high fever, and severe dysuria causing acute urinary retention, requiring urgent physical assessment and catheterization evaluation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient with an abrupt onset of severe localized genital pain, systemic fever, and an inability to pass urine for the last 10 hours. Urgent physical assessment is required to manage acute severe pain, confirm the diagnosis of a primary herpetic outbreak, and rule out or manage acute urinary retention prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I have agonizing, burning sores down below, I feel like I have the flu, and it hurts so much I physically cannot pee."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced 3 days ago with a severe localized burning, intense tingling, and stinging prodrome in the genital region. This was followed 24 hours later by the sudden eruption of multiple highly painful, tightly clustered, thin-walled vesicles situated on a distinctly erythematous base. These have rapidly progressed to shallow, exquisitely tender, and distinctly weeping ulcerations. The patient reports a high fever, intense myalgia, and profound systemic malaise accompanying this primary clinical episode. Crucially, the patient reports severe dysuria that has led to voluntary acute urinary retention; she has not voided in 10 hours due to the excruciating pain of urine contacting the ulcers.
Functional Status: Bedbound, in extreme distress, crying.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; highly distressed by the pain and the inability to void.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 110 | Temp: 38.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Acutely distressed, tearful, flushed, shifting uncomfortably in bed.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals a tender, palpable, distended bladder midway to the umbilicus. Bilateral inguinal regions reveal painful, prominent, and firm regional lymphadenopathy. Genital inspection (performed with consent) reveals multiple clustered vesicles and shallow, exquisitely tender weeping ulcerations on highly inflamed labial mucosa. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Painful clustered vesicles on an erythematous base, Shallow weeping ulcerations, Localized tingling/burning prodrome, and Tender regional lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking paracetamol with no relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Herpes Simplex Virus (Primary Severe Genital Outbreak) - Status: Exacerbated (complicated by acute urinary retention)
Differentials:

Syphilis (Primary Chancre) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The lesions here are exquisitely painful and clustered, whereas a syphilitic chancre is classically a single, painless indurated ulcer.

Chancroid - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of vesicles preceding the ulcers and the prominent systemic viral prodrome (fever, myalgia), though chancroid ulcers are also painful.

Severe Candidiasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of distinct vesicles, weeping ulcerations, and prominent inguinal lymphadenopathy, which are not features of a yeast infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a severe primary HSV outbreak complicated by systemic toxicity and acute urinary retention secondary to extreme dysuria. The inability to void for 10 hours alongside a palpable bladder is a medical emergency that can lead to acute kidney injury or bladder rupture. Immediate on-site clinical evaluation was mandatory to provide potent analgesia, confirm the diagnosis visually, and facilitate safe micturition (or escalate to catheterization). This acute distress and potential for organ damage strictly satisfies the criteria for urgent after-hours intervention (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administer potent oral analgesia immediately. Apply topical lidocaine 5% ointment to the ulcerated areas to acutely numb the mucosa and facilitate voluntary voiding. Prescribe high-dose oral Valacyclovir (e.g., 1g twice daily for 7-10 days) immediately to halt viral replication. Collect a direct multiplex PCR swab from a weeping lesion. If the patient cannot void within 30 minutes of lidocaine application, arrange ED transfer for catheterization.
Positive Behavioral Change: By applying the topical lidocaine 5 minutes before attempting to void and urinating while sitting in a warm bath, the patient will neutralize the burning sensation; achieving painless urination will reward the patient with immediate relief of bladder pressure, reinforcing the use of this pain-management technique.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Sitz bath.
Follow-up: Urgent telehealth review in 12 hours to confirm successful ongoing voiding.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient remains completely unable to pass urine despite the local anesthetic, if they develop severe lower back pain, or experience neck stiffness and severe headache (rare complication of viral meningitis).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is highly confident based on classic clinical presentation; awaiting PCR confirmation.
Clinical Course: The first outbreak is always the most severe, often accompanied by flu-like symptoms. The antiviral medication will rapidly speed up healing, but the ulcers may take 2 weeks to fully resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Your bladder is currently full. If the numbing cream and warm bath trick does not work within the next hour, you must go to the emergency department to have a temporary catheter placed.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The virus causes intense nerve pain and raw sores. Because your urine is acidic, it burns the sores, making your body instinctively clamp down and stop you from peeing. The antiviral pills will stop the virus from multiplying, and the numbing cream will allow you to pee.
Actionable Steps: Start the antiviral pills immediately. Urinate in a warm bath or pour warm water over the area while urinating to dilute the acidity. Wash hands meticulously after touching the area.

